<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Árpád</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 845–c. 907</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Árpád was the leader of the Hungarian tribal federation who, after his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almos">Álmos</a>, led the Hungarians across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carpathian-mountains">Carpathians</a> into the Carpathian Basin around 895. He is regarded as the founder of the Árpád dynasty, which ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> as princes and then kings until 1301.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/arpad.jpg" alt="Árpád" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Csanády · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%C3%81rp%C3%A1d_R%C3%A1ckeve.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Árpád was the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almos">Álmos</a>, whom all Hungarian chronicles name as the first head of the confederation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magyars">Magyar</a> tribes. His mother's name and family are unknown. The historian Gyula Kristó holds that he was born around 845, and his name derives from the Hungarian word for barley, árpa. Most details of his life are debated, because the sources contradict one another. He may have been the sacred ruler, the kende, or the military leader, the gyula. The Byzantine emperor Constantine Porphyrogenitus says the Hungarians had never had another prince before him, and that he was made prince by being lifted on a shield.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>The earliest reliable source says that the Byzantine emperor Leo VI sent his envoy Nicetas Sclerus to the Hungarians in 894 or 895 to incite them against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>. Sclerus met their two leaders, Árpád and Kurszán, on the Lower Danube, and a Hungarian army crossed the river on Byzantine ships and defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgarians">Bulgarians</a>. The Bulgarians then hired the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pechenegs">Pechenegs</a>, and in 895 or 896 the two attacked Hungarian lands in the western Pontic steppes. The destruction of their dwellings forced the Hungarians to cross the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carpathian-mountains">Carpathian Mountains</a> towards the Pannonian Plain.</p><p>Historians disagree about Árpád's position. Sources written in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-francia">East Francia</a> do not know his name and mention only Kurszán, so Kristó and others think Kurszán commanded the forces as gyula while Árpád was the sacred kende. Chronicles written centuries later, such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gesta-hungarorum">Gesta Hungarorum</a>, stress Árpád's preeminent part in the conquest of the Carpathian Basin.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The date of his death is debated, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gesta-hungarorum">Gesta Hungarorum</a> gives 907. Constantine Porphyrogenitus names four sons, Tarkatzous, Ielech, Ioutotzas and Zaltas. Until the male line of his dynasty died out in 1301, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> was ruled by a single line of princes, all descended from Árpád. Hungarians call him honalapító, the founder of our homeland.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Árpád” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>When the last male <b>Árpád</b>, Andrew III, died in January 1301, several candidates descended from the dynasty through the female line claimed the Holy Crown of Hungary.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-hungary-1301-1526">Kingdom of Hungary (1301-1526) · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After the death of Andrew III in 1301 ended the <b>Árpád</b> line, he backed rival claimants and seized castles across north-western Hungary, centred on Trencsén (Trenčín).</blockquote><a href="/state/domain-of-matthew-csak">Domain of Matthew Csák</a></li><li><blockquote>The Gesta says Menumorut refused to cede land to <b>Árpád</b>, claiming the Byzantine emperor as his lord.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-menumorut">Duchy of Menumorut</a></li><li><blockquote>They raided East Francia from 862 and fought for Byzantium against Bulgaria in 894. Álmos and then his son <b>Árpád</b> were elected as leaders.</blockquote><a href="/state/hungarian-tribal-federation">Hungarian tribal federation</a></li><li><blockquote>After Pecheneg attacks drove them from Etelköz, the Hungarian tribal federation under <b>Árpád</b> crossed the Carpathians in 895.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-hungary">Principality of Hungary</a></li><li><blockquote>The gyulas ruled largely independently of the <b>Árpád</b> grand princes, controlling Transylvanian salt and the routes to Byzantium.</blockquote><a href="/state/realm-of-the-gyulas">Realm of the Gyulas</a></li></ul></section>
